From 3b2a5f5f60f9d58594da252d7bf41758fdb5d06b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cindy Krafft <cynthiacondra@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 23:33:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 1.9: --bmerge duplicate-ID reporting (issue #140)

--bmerge keys the merge on variant ID, so several records sharing an ID
describe a single merged variant.  Two log problems followed from counting
records instead of distinct IDs for the second fileset:

* the same fileset reported two different variant counts depending on
  whether it was the base ("N markers loaded from") or the second fileset
  ("N markers to be merged from"), and
* "Of these, X are new, while Y are present in the base dataset" could
  report more variants present in the base than the base dataset has.

The duplicate-ID warning was also restricted to the first fileset, which is
the asymmetry the existing comment at that site describes and leaves as a
todo: in the reporter's case the fileset with the duplicate IDs was silently
accepted when it was the second one.

merge_bim_scan() now stamps each hash-table entry with the index of the last
fileset it was seen in, so a duplicate ID *within* the current fileset is
distinguishable from an ID that also appears in an earlier fileset, and
reports that count to merge_datasets().  The warning fires for whichever
fileset has the duplicates and names it, and the second fileset's variant
count is its distinct-ID count.  Genotype output is unchanged.

New test: 2.0/Tests/TEST_BMERGE_DUP_IDS.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
